```java
Optional<User> user = findUser(id);

// Won't compile: Optional<User> has no .getName() -- you must unwrap first
// user.getName();

// Forced to handle both cases explicitly:
if (user.isPresent()) {
    System.out.println(user.get().getName());
} else {
    System.out.println("No user found");
}

// Or, more idiomatically, without ever calling the unsafe get():
String name = user.map(User::getName).orElse("No user found");
```

The critical detail: `Optional<User>` genuinely does not expose `User`'s methods directly — trying `user.getName()` is a compile error, not a runtime risk, because `Optional` and `User` are different types with different members. You're forced to either check `isPresent()` and then call `.get()` inside that branch, or use combinators like `.map()`/`.orElse()` that handle the absent case as part of the expression itself, with no branch to forget.

The second style is preferred specifically because it makes forgetting the absent case a type error rather than a possibility — there's no code path where a present-only assumption sneaks through unchecked.
